**PROVED (L’s scope).** Independently draw each $x_j$ uniformly outside $S_j$. Avoiding the forbidden set shifts the mean of a bounded statistic by at most $2|S_j|/q$. Hoeffding plus a union bound controls all $Q$ statistics with positive probability at the stated scale; the condition $J^2\le q/4$ leaves positive probability of distinctness as well. These fake zero-hit configurations need not satisfy the actual reciprocal-prime identities. That last distinction is the available escape.

### 7.2 Ward–Maestro floors

**KNOWN (restricted models).** [Ward–Maestro, *Floors for deterministic integer factoring*](https://igent.ai/research/floors-not-bounds/factoring_report.pdf), Sept. 2026:

| Scope | Assumptions ⇒ floor; exact locator |
|---|---|
| **KNOWN: cells** | Positive integer forms $aq+bp$; real-range coverage; tested integer sets $T_i$, $W_i=\vert T_i\vert $. Materialized $T\ge m+\sum_i\lceil W_i/m\rceil$; free $T\ge m+\sum_iW_i/m$. Floors $N^{1/5},N^{1/6}$: Definition 2.1, Theorems 3.4/3.6. |
| **KNOWN: thinning/robustness** | Enumerated residue hypotheses, modulus $q\le N^{1/20}$: $N^{1/5}(\varphi(q)/q)^{3/5}$, Theorem 2.8. Fixed-degree forms: Theorem 9.4; adaptive cell-membership coverage: Proposition 9.5. |
| **KNOWN: explicit differences** | Explicit $B-G$ cover; start-statistic envelope $D_{\max}\le r^\eta$: $N^{(1-\eta)/(5-4\eta)}$, Theorem 4.4. Polynomial-size divisibility objects: $N^{1/4}$, Proposition 4.5. |
| **KNOWN: pieces/localization** | Separate pieces priced $\ge\sqrt{W_j}$, $H=\max(1,\max_j\sum_{i\in I_j:\,a_i\le b_i\le\lceil Ca_i\rceil}1/a_i)$: $N^{1/4}/\sqrt H$, Theorem 10.1. M1, $N^{1/4}\le L\le\sqrt N$: $\min(LN^{-1/4}/\operatorname{polylog}N,L^{3/5}N^{-1/10})$, Theorem 13.2; position/depth refinement, Theorem 13.15. |
| **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE: NFS** | Uniform root, $H<\min(p,q)$: probability $\le d^2(2H+1)^{d+1}/N$, Proposition 38.1. Coprime common-root resultant floor: Proposition 40.1. Equidistributed-root **heuristic**: $\min(\Vertf\Vert_\infty m)=N^{(2d-1)/(d^2+d-1)+o(1)}$; GNFS-constant optimum, Propositions 39.1/42.1. |
| **KNOWN: other parts** | Fixed-list ECM exposure criterion: Proposition 32.4, not a universal lower bound. Common-($p-1,q-1$) algorithms: Proposition 28.2; positive-density $p-1$ success: Proposition 35.3. |

**PROVED (independent resultant inequality).** If coprime integer polynomials $f,g$ of degrees $d,e$ share a root modulo $N$, their nonzero resultant is divisible by $N$: the Sylvester resultant identity specializes to zero at that root. Hadamard’s determinant bound gives

$$
N\le|\operatorname{Res}(f,g)|
\le (d+1)^{e/2}(e+1)^{d/2}\|f\|_\infty^e\|g\|_\infty^d.
$$

This elementary inequality survives fast factoring. It constrains these explicit polynomial pairs, not all ways of finding a factor. A bound on coefficients alone does not prohibit a different relation architecture or improve an analytic smoothness estimate.

**PROVED (how to read this together with the repository).** The supplied notes’ full-cover and local-pair models are different quantifiers. D′ can beat a materialized single-family floor while obeying a broader $1/6$ floor. Supplied factor information changes the search problem, as the Coppersmith example below illustrates. A faster algorithm that never constructs the tested cell families has no obligation to violate their geometric lemmas.

### 7.3 Which known algorithm families have which escapes?

The labels in the running-time column separate established algorithms from unproved analyses. “Outside” means the model’s definition does not contain that method, not that a theorem has been disproved.

| Family | Coverage/locality and information | Relations/order information | Determinism and bound |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pollard–Strassen / BGS products | **PROVED.** Divisibility by interval products, not a degree-one true-factor selector. Dense degree grows; product trees give a square-root saving in interval length. | **PROVED.** Direct nonunit detection; no need for smooth $p-1$. | **KNOWN.** Deterministic $N^{1/4+o(1)}$ general bound; see Bostan–Gaudry–Schost, SIAM J. Comput. 36 (2007), **Theorem 5** ([primary paper](https://mathexp.eu/bostan/publications/BoGaSc07.pdf)) for the evaluation ingredient, as used in [Track C §1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). It supplies no sub-$1/6$ general algorithm. |
| Harvey / Harvey–Hittmeir | **KNOWN.** Explicit degree-one collision targets and arithmetic thinning. Harvey, Math. Comp. 90 (2021), **Theorem 1.1**; HH, *A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation*, Math. Comp. 91 (2022), **Theorem 1.1**. | **PROVED.** Collision identity uses $p-1$, but not only a fixed list of smooth-order annihilators. | **KNOWN.** Worst-case exponent $1/5$; the refinement improves logarithms. [Harvey](https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/3658). |
| D′ / AV | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Many-modulus selective coverage, within T1 after the stated budget domination. | **PROVED.** Shared collision machinery; average coverage and extraction, not merely a fixed smooth-order list. | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Deterministic $N^{1/6}\operatorname{polylog}N$ mean on uniform $S_n$, with $N^{1/5}$ fallback; [AV](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/avgtime_results.md), [D′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dcltheorem_results.md). It attains, rather than escapes, the local floor. |
| Pollard $p-1$, ECM | **PROVED.** Success is group-order/point-order divisibility, outside cell coverage. ECM varies curves. | **PROVED.** One-sided annihilation must separate the two prime components. Fixed-list certificates are covered by §8; input-dependent choices need not be. | **SPECULATIVE at the usual subexponential ECM bound.** Its familiar expected $L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$ analysis needs the relevant smooth-order supply. A positive-density $p-1$ theorem is not an almost-all or worst-case factorizer. |
| QS / NFS | **PROVED.** Relation collection and global linear algebra, outside the charged sumset-cover definition. | **PROVED.** Build a square congruence from dependencies; fruitfulness is an additional issue. | **SPECULATIVE.** The standard QS $L[1/2]$, GNFS $L[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}]$, and straightforward deterministic enumerations with those costs rely on relation-supply/fruitfulness assumptions. A heuristic deterministic candidate family can have the desired structural signature; it is not a proved deterministic bound. |
| Schnorr–Lenstra / Seysen / LP | **KNOWN.** Quadratic-class-group relation methods are outside local Lehman coverage. Seysen, Math. Comp. 48 (1987), **Proposition 8.6 / Theorem 8.7**, gives the subgroup-based extraction context; LP’s **§1 main theorem** gives the unconditional randomized $L[1/2,1]$ bound. | **PROVED.** Smooth ideals, relations, and ambiguous forms; not an identity $C_N^{h(p,q)}=D_N$ with a known short true-factor exponent. | **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE.** LP’s randomized bound is rigorous; the earlier analyses have their own GRH or smoothness assumptions. Deterministically enumerating the random choices does not preserve the expectation bound. |
| Coppersmith | **KNOWN.** Given roughly $\frac14\log_2N$ high or low bits of a factor, polynomial-time lattice decoding factors $N=pq$: Coppersmith, J. Cryptology 10 (1997), **Theorems 4–5**. These are outside full-range cell coverage. [Article](https://doi.org/10.1007/s001459900030). | **PROVED.** Uses arithmetic smallness and lattices, not only group orders. | **PROVED conditional use.** Polynomial decoding with a sufficiently precise supplied hint is not a polynomial algorithm that obtains the hint. |
| Shor | **KNOWN.** Quantum order finding and measurement, outside classical collision accounting and classical generic-query bounds. Shor, SIAM J. Comput. 26 (1997), **§§4–5, order-finding/factoring algorithms**. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027). | **PROVED.** Supplies an exact order via a quantum procedure, then uses the classical order-to-factor reduction. | **KNOWN.** Randomized quantum polynomial time; not a classical deterministic escape. |

**PROVED (intersection of necessary escapes).** For a classical deterministic factorizer with worst-case cost $N^{1/6-\delta}$, certified local T1/T2/T3 coverage cannot remain its sole success certificate. If it still uses cell coverage, it must also leave the appropriate full-cover/cost model; if it uses the O1/A1 sketches, it must leave their linear/dense-representation premises. These are **conditional membership tests**, not a demand that every algorithm break every row. A relation algorithm can avoid the entire cell family. A group-only algorithm can avoid cell locality yet still face an unresolved deterministic order-supply or separation problem.

**SPECULATIVE (which family is closest).** No family in this table has a **proved classical deterministic** worst-case or balanced-almost-all $N^{1/6-\delta}$ guarantee. QS/NFS are the clearest structurally compatible candidates; LP shows that a relation/class-group escape can already be made rigorous with randomness. Shor meets a stronger speed goal by changing the computational model. This is an audit conclusion about the listed families, not a proof that an unlisted family cannot work.

## 8. A proved combined barrier, and its limits

### 8.1 A quantitative extension of the T1 locality calculation

Define the key enlargement parameter

$$
\kappa=\max_\iota\max(1,K_\iota/M_\iota^2).
$$

**PROVED (key-enlargement lemma).** Retain T1’s fixed generators, $M\mid K$, $M,K\le P/2$, normalized $D/|A|\ll M^2$, and its charged budgets; drop only the bound $K\ll M^2$. The number of covered prime pairs in any fixed balanced subbox is at most

$$
C P^{3/2}\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}.
\tag{8.1}
$$

Consequently the covered fraction in a prime-pair box of cardinality $\gg P^2/n^2$ is at most

$$
C n^2\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}.
\tag{8.2}
$$

Proof: fix the class prime $x$ and key $c=N\bmod K$. Its possible partner integers form one progression modulo $K$. Each permitted target value confines that partner to an interval of length $D/|A|$; the entire partner range has length $O(P)$. If the window has $s$ values, the count is at most

$$
\min\!\left(s\left(\frac{D}{|A|K}+1\right),\frac{CP}{K}\right)
\le \frac{C\sqrt{Ps(D/|A|+K)}}K.
$$

There are $O(P/M)$ class-variable integers per allowed residue. Summing by keys and comparing each key with its frequency among all $n$-bit inputs gives

$$
\#\text{pairs for a generator}
\le C\frac{P^{3/2}}M\sqrt{D/|A|+K}\,
\mathbb E_{\rm all\ inputs}(|R|\sqrt s)
\le CP^{3/2}\sqrt\kappa\,\mathbb E(|R|\sqrt s).
$$

Sum over generators, counting each allowed class as a target, and apply Cauchy–Schwarz:

$$
\mathbb E\sum_\tau\sqrt{|S_\tau|}
\le\sqrt{(\mathbb E\#\tau)(\mathbb E\sum|S_\tau|)}\le X^{3/2}.
$$

For a one-term target, swap the two prime variables and transform its class by $r\mapsto cr^{-1}\pmod M$; it becomes the nonzero-partner-coefficient case, with no larger class set. This proves (8.1), and division gives (8.2). The source of the expectation is **all $n$-bit integers**, just as in T1; an average over semiprimes alone cannot be substituted.

**PROVED (a necessary size for this escape).** If this enlarged-key model covers a constant fraction at cost $N^{1/6-\delta}$, then

$$
\kappa\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N.
\tag{8.3}
$$

Substitute into (8.2) and solve. Merely replacing a local key by another key of comparable size does not escape the exponent.

### 8.2 Local coverage plus fixed group-order exposure

The following is one precise interpretation of “local in sense X and uses only group-order information in sense Y.” It is deliberately narrower than arbitrary access to exact group orders.

**Model X.** Certified local hits are those of the key-enlarged T1 model above, with budget $X$. Any continuation after unsuccessful exposure tests must still satisfy that model; failures cannot secretly choose unrestricted global weights.

**Model Y.** The extra mechanism uses a public, input-independent list of integer vanishing certificates $V_1,\ldots,V_t$, represented in any way whose evaluation costs are charged. It can split only by the gcds of these values with $N$. Its prime signature is

$$
\sigma(p)=(1_{p\mid V_1},\ldots,1_{p\mid V_t}).
$$

More generally any fixed-list order procedure is allowed if its successful splitting **requires different signatures** for the two prime components. Let $R$ be the number of distinct signatures on the relevant primes. The certificates may be huge, evaluated modulo $N$; their *prime dependence* must be fixed independently of $N=pq$. Fixed-base $p-1$ annihilators and fixed-curve, fixed-bound ECM atoms are examples after denominators have been included as certificates.

**PROVED (combined local/exposure theorem).** Choose a prime interval $I\subset[cP,CP]$ of fixed relative length, with $\Pi\gg P/n$ primes, so that all products of distinct primes in $I$ lie in one $n$-bit balanced range. On uniform unordered pairs from this interval, an X+Y algorithm failing on at most a fraction ε satisfies

$$
C n^2\sqrt\kappa\,X^{3/2}N^{-1/4}
\ge \frac1R-\varepsilon-O(1/\Pi).
\tag{8.4}
$$

In particular, if it succeeds on **every** pair, $R=o(\Pi)$, then

$$
X\gg
\frac{N^{1/6}}{\kappa^{1/3}R^{2/3}n^{4/3}}.
\tag{8.5}
$$

Proof: partition the $\Pi$ primes by signature, with class sizes $a_1,\ldots,a_R$. The equal-signature pair fraction is

$$
\frac{\sum_j a_j(a_j-1)}{\Pi(\Pi-1)}
\ge\frac{\Pi/R-1}{\Pi-1}
\ge\frac1R-O(1/\Pi),
$$

using $\sum a_j^2\ge\Pi^2/R$. On each such pair, every certificate gcd is 1 or $N$, so Model Y cannot split. Apart from the allowed failures, Model X must cover these pairs. Apply (8.2). Rearranging proves (8.5). The prime-count assumption is the usual fixed-proportion interval consequence of PNT; it can also simply be retained as an explicit cardinality hypothesis of the finite theorem. No distributional assertion about smooth group orders is used.

**PROVED (escape inequality).** For worst-case $X=N^{1/6-\delta}$, the theorem requires

$$
\kappa R^2\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N.
\tag{8.6}
$$

For the original locality bound $\kappa=O(1)$, this means $R\ge N^{3\delta/2}/\operatorname{polylog}N$. Since binary tests have $R\le2^t$, at least $(3\delta/2)\log_2N-O(\log\log N)$ binary tests are necessary; this is a cardinality requirement, not an independence assertion. For polynomial $X$ and bounded κ, the corresponding condition is $R\ge N^{1/4}/\operatorname{polylog}N$.

**PROVED (almost-all qualification).** If $\kappa,R=N^{o(1)}$ and $\varepsilon=o(1/R)$, the same theorem gives the $N^{1/6-o(1)}$ floor. An unspecified ε $=o(1)$ is insufficient when $R\to\infty$: all remaining equal-signature pairs might fit inside that exceptional set. With constant $R$, ε $=o(1)$ suffices. This distinction prevents a false upgrade from worst case to almost all.

**PROVED (limits).** This theorem does not outlaw a polynomial-time algorithm: $t=\Theta(n)$ can already allow exponentially many signatures. It also does not cover $N$-dependent exponents or curves, full exact order outputs, hidden cross-correlations between the local and order stages, useful accidental collisions, or a relation algorithm. Extending it to those cases requires additional mathematics. It is a double-counting theorem, not an unrestricted computational lower bound.

**FALSE (a broader formulation).** “Locality plus any use of group orders implies an $N^{1/6}$ lower bound.” Without a precise restriction on what the order information is allowed to reveal, the statement includes an exact-order oracle, which factors by §3.2, and includes smooth-order methods lying outside T1. The correct combined statement is (8.4), with its explicit premises.

## 9. Q3(i–ii): independent blockers and useful reverse reductions

### 9.1 Which consequences deserve the word blocker?

| Hypothesis or mechanism | Established consequence | Strength as a blocker |
|---|---|---|
| H1 | **PROVED.** Deterministic polynomial μ, squarefreeness, σ, φ, radicals, maximal orders, residuosity decision, and the listed composite-modulus counts. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** These settle recognized algorithmic problems but do not conflict with their mathematical definitions or existing lower bounds. They are checkpoints for a candidate, not independent impossibility evidence. |
| H2 | **PROVED.** Randomized versions of the above and polynomial randomized order finding. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** No analytic conjecture or complexity collapse beyond those reductions was identified. It refutes the relevant factoring-hardness assumptions. |
| Rigorous H3 | **PROVED.** A new proved subexponential factoring upper bound. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** That does not require proving GNFS smoothness or NFS fruitfulness unless the proposed algorithm uses them. For a GNFS-like proof, those are genuine mechanism-dependent open inputs. |
| Worst-case H4 via Theorem R | **PROVED in repository framework.** The proposed coverage certificate must meet U’s low-$\lambda$ threshold; its all-coprime-pair version implies the sparse $U(x,H)$ statement of B3. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** This is a sharper, noncircular obstacle for that mechanism. The sparse union statement is not a known prime-gap conjecture and need not follow from an unrelated H4 algorithm. |
| Sub-$1/6$ H4 within X+Y | **PROVED.** The inequalities (8.3)/(8.6) force quantified key enlargement or many prime signatures, or departure from X+Y. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** Cheap and decisive for a precisely specified candidate. It does not rule out relation methods or unrestricted bit computation. |
| H5 squarefreeness | **KNOWN, BL Theorem 6.12.** Polynomial squarefreeness gives polynomial maximal-order recognition. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** A separate useful open algorithmic problem; not a verified reduction to general factoring. |
| H5 full uniform totient residues | **PROVED, §9.2.** CRT gives exact φ, and the semiprime formula factors. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** A strong warning that “small outputs” can collectively encode the entire search problem. |
| A uniformly short exact-factorial circuit mechanism | **PROVED, §5.1.** It solves modular factorial thresholds and factoring; published arithmetic-complexity implications involve still stronger nonuniform hypotheses. | **SPECULATIVE assessment.** This is a useful additional obligation of the mechanism. Fast factoring alone does not force it. |

**PROVED (no automatic NP collapse).** Factoring a positive binary integer is a total search problem with an efficiently checked, unique sorted prime-power answer. Its threshold decision version also has certificates for the “no small factor” case: provide the complete prime factorization and primality certificates. A polynomial-time factoring algorithm would solve this problem; it does not, without an NP-hardness reduction, solve all of NP. None of the reductions in this report supplies such a reduction. The same reasoning applies to a claim that H1 proves $P=BPP$: solving one randomized problem does not derandomize every other one.

### 9.2 Small totient residues: a complete reduction and a weak bit

**PROVED (uniform small-modulus CRT reduction).** Suppose an oracle, for a fixed input $N$, returns $\varphi(N)\bmod m$ for every requested prime modulus $m\le Cn$, with fixed sufficiently large $C$. Take distinct such primes until their product $M>N$. There are $O(n/\log n)$ of them by the prime number theorem, their enumeration is polynomial time, and CRT reconstructs the unique integer $0\le\varphi(N)<N$. For $N=pq$, §3.2 then factors deterministically. On arbitrary $N$, the multiple-of-λ reduction gives randomized polynomial-time factoring. The bit cost of the CRT stage is polynomial in $n$.

**PROVED (elementary alternative without prime-density estimates).** Query moduli $2,3,\ldots,2n+2$, or just their maximal prime powers. Their lcm exceeds $N$: $\operatorname{lcm}(1,\ldots,k)\ge2^{k-1}/k$ follows because each binomial coefficient $\binom{k-1}{j}$ divides that lcm, and the largest coefficient is at least $2^{k-1}/k$. With $k=2n+2$ this exceeds $2^n$ for all sufficiently large $n$; finitely many smaller inputs are handled directly. Consistent residue reconstruction modulo the lcm therefore also recovers φ using only $O(n)$ moduli of size $O(n)$. To justify the divisibility assertion, for each prime $p$, $v_p\binom{k-1}{j}$ counts carries in base $p$ and is at most $\lfloor\log_p k\rfloor$, the exponent available in the lcm. Alternatively this bound follows immediately by subtracting the three factorial-valuation sums.

**PROVED (balanced sum reconstruction).** For $p<q\le2p$, $2\sqrt N<s=p+q\le(3/\sqrt2)\sqrt N$. Thus $s\bmod M$ with $M>(3/\sqrt2-2)\sqrt N+O(1)$ leaves at most one possible integer $s$ in the public interval. Knowing φ modulo such an $M$ supplies $s\equiv N+1-\varphi(N)\pmod M$, and the discriminant factors $N$. Fewer residues may feed a lattice decoder instead, but the shape and ambiguity must be charged.

**PROVED (one fixed bit is different).** For distinct odd $p,q$ with $N\equiv1\pmod4$,

$$
\varphi(N)\equiv
\begin{cases}
0\pmod8,&p\equiv q\equiv1\pmod4,\\
4\pmod8,&p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod4.
\end{cases}
$$

Hence φ modulo 8 is exactly the ε/two-square distinction on this promise. The calculation uses $(p-1)(q-1)$; φ modulo 2 or 4 supplies no such distinction on this promise. This equivalence of **bits** does not turn one modulo-8 oracle into the uniform CRT oracle.

**PROVED (2-adic hint geometry).** If $v=v_2(q-p)$, solutions to $x^2-sx+N=0\pmod{2^k}$ are determined by $v_2(x-p)+v_2(x-q)\ge k$. For $k>2v$, they are two cosets modulo $2^{k-v}$; for $k\le2v$, one coset modulo $2^{\lceil k/2\rceil}$. For $v_2(x-p)<v$, both valuations are equal; above $v$, the other valuation is exactly $v$, proving the alternatives. This explains the $v$-bit loss in [Track E, Proposition E2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackE_analysis.md). A sufficient known-bits decoder threshold is not an information-theoretic necessity theorem.

### 9.3 μ, squarefreeness, ω, and the divisor function

**PROVED (the easy decision arrows).** A full μ oracle decides squarefreeness by $\mu(N)\ne0$. A radical oracle does so by $\operatorname{rad}(N)=N$. Ordinary Jacobi symbols require no factoring oracle: strip factors of 2 using $(2/b)=(-1)^{(b^2-1)/8}$, swap odd arguments using quadratic reciprocity, and reduce the numerator modulo the denominator. Euclidean descent uses $O(\log N)$ steps on $O(\log N)$-bit integers. This supplies an unconditional deterministic polynomial-time algorithm; an unknown-factor Legendre bit is a different interface.

**PROVED (why the semiprime promise matters).** On distinct-prime semiprimes, $\omega(N)=2$, $\Omega(N)=2$, $d(N)=4$, $\mu(N)=1$, squarefreeness is true, and $\operatorname{rad}(N)=N$. These functions on **that promise alone** reveal no factor. An all-integer oracle can query transformed inputs; proving a useful transformation is the missing reduction, not a consequence of the constant promise values.

**PROVED (one divisor-count query computes μ).** Choose a prime $q>1+\log_2N$ of size $O(\log N)$, deterministically by small-number enumeration. Ask for

$$
D=d(N^{q-1})=\prod_{p^a\parallel N}(1+(q-1)a).
$$

Every exponent $a<q$. A factor in this product is divisible by $q$ iff $a=1$, in which case it equals $q$. If any $a>1$, its factor is $>1$ and coprime to $q$. Therefore $D$ is a pure power $q^r$ iff $N$ is squarefree; then μ $=(-1)^r$, otherwise μ $=0$. All inputs and outputs have polynomial bit length. This proves the direction associated with Shallit–Shamir, *Number-theoretic functions which are equivalent to number of divisors*, IPL 20 (1985), pp. 151–153, without needing an unverified theorem number. [Original abstract](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(85)90084-5).

**PROVED (exponent histogram from divisor counts).** Put $r\le n$ for the number of prime factors and $Q(t)=d(N^t)=\prod_i(1+e_it)$, a degree-$r$ polynomial. Values at $t=0,\ldots,n$ recover $Q$ by exact rational interpolation. For each $a=1,\ldots,n$, repeatedly divide by $1+at$; the multiplicity recovered is the number of primes of exponent $a$. This computes any efficiently postprocessed function of the exponent multiset. It identifies no prime labels. The queried integers have up to $O(n^2)$ bits, so this is a polynomial-bit reduction, not a preservation of an H4 numerical exponent.

**PROVED (radical from an exact φ oracle).** Set $m_0=N$, and recursively

$$
g_i=\gcd(m_i,\varphi(m_i)),\qquad r_i=m_i/g_i,\qquad m_{i+1}=g_i.
$$

Since $v_p(\varphi(m_i))\ge v_p(m_i)-1$, each $r_i$ is squarefree. For $m_i>1$, $g_i<m_i$, so there are at most $\log_2N$ iterations. The product of the $r_i$ is $N$, and consequently $\operatorname{rad}(N)=\operatorname{lcm}_i r_i$. This proves a deterministic polynomial-time **Turing** reduction radical ≤ φ, and hence squarefreeness ≤ φ. It is the direction relevant to Landau’s *Some remarks on computing the square parts of integers*, Inf. Comput. 78 (1988), and Woll’s *Reductions among number theoretic problems*, Inf. Comput. 72 (1987); no reverse factoring reduction from a squarefreeness bit is being claimed. [Landau](https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(88)90028-4), [Woll](https://doi.org/10.1016/0890-5401(87)90030-7).

**PROVED (squarefree decomposition from radicals).** Repeatedly divide a remaining integer by its radical. A prime occurring with exponent $e$ appears in exactly the first $e$ radical layers. Ratios of successive layers give the products of primes with each exact exponent. Thus full radical construction yields squarefree decomposition in polynomially many calls, but a decision-only squarefreeness oracle does not supply these layers by this proof.

**PROVED (a broad useless family of special shapes).** For squarefree $N=pq$, $\gcd(N,k)=1$, multiplicativity gives $\mu(Nk)=\mu(k)$, and (Nk) is squarefree iff $k$ is squarefree. For $k>1$, $\mu(Nk^2)=0$. Likewise $\omega(Nk)=2+\omega(k)$. Consequently querying these coprime multiplicative masks, whose own factorization is known, cannot extract a factor of $N$. If a mask is not coprime, its gcd with $N$ already supplies a factor. This is a concrete kill criterion for one family of μ/squarefreeness-oracle proposals.

**SPECULATIVE (remaining reverse reductions).** This audit does not establish general polynomial reductions factoring ≤ μ, factoring ≤ squarefreeness decision, factoring ≤ ω, factoring ≤ two-square decision, or factoring ≤ quadratic-residuosity decision in the ordinary bit model. Neither the σ theorem nor the divisor-count-to-μ construction supplies these arrows. “Not established” is not an oracle-separation theorem or a proof of impossibility.

**SPECULATIVE (historical citation audit).** I did not verify an exact Woll theorem asserting a relativized separation involving μ or squarefreeness; the checked reduction is consistent with the φ direction proved above. Adleman–McCurley’s *Open problems in number theoretic complexity, II*, ANTS 1994, is a historical problem list, not a theorem closing these gaps; no unverified problem number is used here. [Original chapter](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-58691-1_70). These are explicit limits of this literature audit.

**KNOWN (a useful reverse point-counting theorem).** Dąbrowski–Pomykała–Shparlinski, *On oracle factoring of integers*, J. Complexity 76 (2023), 101741, **Definition 1.1 / Theorem 1.2**, use the counts $E(m,b)=\prod_{p\mid m}\#E_b(\mathbb F_p)$ for $E_b:y^2=x(x^2-b)$. Their oracle returns polynomially bounded positive multiples for squarefree $m$ and $\gcd(b,m)=1$, and signals nonsquarefreeness otherwise. For $2<\gamma<2+(\sqrt{257}-15)/16$, $0<d<1/4$, and $b\le(\log Z)^\gamma$, a deterministic algorithm finds a factor for all but $O(Z/(\log Z)^d)$ integers $m\le Z$, using $O((\log Z)^\gamma)$ queries and time

$$
O((\log Z)^{\rho(\gamma)+o(1)}),\qquad
\rho(\gamma)=\max\{\gamma+2,(9\gamma-17)/(8(\gamma-2))\}.
$$

The oracle bound is $Z^{O(1)}$; polynomial oracle time is an assumption. **Theorem 1.4** gives a one-query product-count variant with a weaker exceptional set. [Primary paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.00345).

**PROVED (distributional limit of that theorem).** An exceptional set of size $Z/(\log Z)^d$, $d<1/4$, is large enough to contain every balanced semiprime in a fixed dyadic prime box, whose size is $\asymp Z/(\log Z)^2$. Therefore this theorem alone does not imply an almost-all **balanced-semiprime** reduction. It nevertheless supplies a precise family of point-count outputs for reverse-reduction experiments.

### 9.4 A harmless consequence that is directly usable: testing fruitfulness

**PROVED (linear-algebra decision-to-search).** Let $A\in\mathbb F_2^{d\times r}$ encode relation parities, and let $c\in\mathbb F_2^r$ encode a valid computable fruitfulness functional on its kernel. There exists a dependency $v$ with $Av=0$, $c\cdot v=1$, iff

$$
c\notin\operatorname{rowspan}(A),
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}\!\begin{bmatrix}A\\c\end{bmatrix}=\operatorname{rank}(A)+1.
\tag{9.1}
$$

Proof: $(\ker A)^\perp=\operatorname{rowspan}(A)$. Gaussian elimination either expresses $c$ in that rowspace or constructs the indicated $v$ by solving the augmented system. This is a polynomial-time **deterministic** search once a suitable relation system is present. On the Blum/QS and degree-one setups where the repository proves the computable Jacobi row, it is directly operational. It does not prove that relation generation ever produces the rank increase.

**PROVED (a harmless symmetry, with its exact premise).** Suppose a relation transcript and dependency selection depend on a sampled root $u$ only through $u^2\bmod N$. Condition on that transcript and all other randomness. Suppose the chosen dependency uses this root to an odd power, so one extracted square root is (uW) for a fixed unit $W$, while the other root is fixed by the transcript. The first root is uniform in the common CRT fibre, and the second is fixed; success is $1-2^{1-\omega(N)}$ by §3.3. This is the Dixon-fibre mechanism in [LemmaM, Theorem DD](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lemmaM_results.md). Revealing a sign-sensitive predicate or choosing the dependency from hidden sign data destroys the premise. Global uniformity of the square is unnecessary.

## 10. Q3(iii): ranked research directions

The ranking favors a short decisive calculation or lemma over a broad new factoring program. Feasibility estimates are **SPECULATIVE** judgments, not numerical probabilities.

### Rank 1 — audit the key size of a genuinely input-dependent selector

**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for a decisive audit, low to moderate for a positive escape).** Specify one selector that inspects more of $N$ than $N\bmod O(M^2)$, while retaining a computable collision certificate. Measure its key size $K$, classes, windows, and evaluation cost on the same input distribution. Start with selectors based on several cross-modulus residues or a short continued-fraction prefix. The object to test is the conditional placement of useful windows, rather than another unconditional correlation statistic.

**PROVED (first lemma already available).** The key-enlargement lemma (8.1) says that gaining a fixed exponent $\delta$ through this particular escape requires $\kappa\ge N^{3\delta}/\operatorname{polylog}N$. Thus a proposed selector with $K=M^2N^{o(1)}$ cannot beat the exponent in this model. For a concrete large-key selector, the next lemma to prove is that its certified coverage gain survives the charged cost of constructing the key-dependent windows.

**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Kill the proposal if its key can be compressed back into $O(M^2)N^{o(1)}$ without changing the targets, if it needs factor information to place the windows, or if its extra construction cost cancels the claimed exponent saving. A one-page key/cost certificate should precede numerical success rates.

### Rank 2 — classify partial-information reductions on promise-preserving shapes

**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for exclusions, low for a general reduction).** Treat ε, φ modulo 8, arbitrary QR decision, and uniform small-modulus φ as distinct oracles. Search for a polynomial-bit transformation $N\mapsto N_j$ that preserves the two-distinct-prime, $1\bmod4$ promise while turning an ε answer into a useful bit about the original factors. Separately test additive shapes for μ/squarefreeness; coprime multiplicative masks are already excluded by §9.3.

**PROVED (first lemma and baseline).** On the promised semiprimes, ε is equivalent to φ modulo 8 by §9.2. Uniform φ residues reconstruct full φ. A claimed interpolation between these endpoints must therefore state exactly which moduli or transformed inputs it generates. One valid next lemma would be a reduction of general Jacobi-$+1$ residuosity to ε on explicitly constructed semiprimes, with the promise and query bit lengths proved. This is a research target, not a supplied reduction.

**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Reject transformations whose outputs require finding fresh factors, whose promise verification is as hard as the target, or whose answers reduce algebraically to known-mask data. Record oracle-query growth: replacing an $n$-bit query by an $n^2$-bit one preserves polynomial-bit complexity but destroys a claimed preservation of an H4 numerical exponent.

### Rank 3 — force a relation-space rank increase with a certified sampling symmetry

**SPECULATIVE (proposal; moderate feasibility for a toy construction, low for a new rigorous asymptotic bound).** Use the computable Jacobi/fruitfulness row where the notes establish it. Generate relations while tracking whether that row leaves the parity rowspace. Seek a sampling rule invariant under hidden CRT sign changes, with coefficients small enough to retain an NFS-like advantage. Global equidistribution of roots is stronger than needed; invariance within a fibre is the relevant premise.

**PROVED (first lemma and measurable target).** Equation (9.1) converts fruitful-dependency search into one rank comparison, and the fibre calculation supplies the success law if its symmetry premise holds. The next necessary lemma is quantitative: the proposed small-coefficient relation sampler produces the rank increase at a stated frequency after conditioning on the visible transcript. Merely observing a large kernel does not establish it.

**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Stop if the fruitfulness row always lies in the parity rowspace for the selected relation family, if sign-sensitive filtering destroys the fibre argument, or if enforcing the symmetry expands coefficients to the Dixon scale and erases the intended gain. Charge relation supply and descent separately; a fast linear-algebra stage cannot compensate for absent useful relations.

### Rank 4 — prove a residual-tail theorem for a deterministic order-exposure portfolio

**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for finite falsification, moderate for an average-case lemma, low for worst case).** Fix inexpensive Pollard-$p-1$ and fixed-curve ECM tests, then use D′ or a deterministic fallback on unexposed inputs. Count prime-signature collisions and the residual semiprime mass, rather than the marginal success probability of a single test. The finite task is to identify which residual patterns survive every test; the mathematical task is a uniform bound on their mass.

**PROVED (first quantitative lemma).** If the initial stage costs at most $N^a$, its residual fraction is $\eta$, and the fallback costs at most $N^b$, the mean is at most $N^a+\eta N^b$. With $b=1/5$, this bound certifies a target mean $N^{1/6-\delta+o(1)}$ when $a\le1/6-\delta$ and

$$
\eta\le N^{-1/30-\delta+o(1)}.
$$

A fixed positive proportion of successful order tests cannot certify this exponent by the displayed estimate alone. A faster conditional fallback mean could alter the estimate. Equal-signature classes satisfy the exact pair-count constraint in §8; their empirical histogram is a useful diagnostic but is not a proof of a tail bound.

**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** Kill a claimed mean-exponent gain if the surviving fraction stays bounded away from zero, if it is only known to be $o(1)$ with no rate, or if the fallback distribution has not been bounded. An exceptionally difficult finite residual family is useful evidence for redesigning the portfolio, not an asymptotic lower bound.

### Rank 5 — test whether batched large-prime-factor search beats enumeration

**SPECULATIVE (proposal; high feasibility for a decisive cost audit, low for a positive construction).** Seek a batched predicate that locates a large prime factor somewhere in a short interval without separately factoring every integer. This is the missing ingredient in the prime-construction route, rather than a better individual factorizer. Require ordinary binary inputs and charge the formation and reading of every product.

**PROVED (first lemma and threshold).** If a theorem guarantees a factor $>X^\theta$, and the **entire** search and extraction costs $X^{b+o(1)}$, the resulting prime has lower scale $z=X^\theta$, time $z^{b/\theta+o(1)}$, and upper scale $z^{1/\theta+o(1)}$. To beat the $z^{1/2}$ baseline one needs $b<\theta/2$. If extraction alone costs $X^\alpha$, and batching costs $X^\xi$, the necessary condition is $\max(\xi,\alpha)<\theta/2$, rather than $\beta+\alpha<\theta/2$. This separates the batching target from the enumeration calculation in §4.

**SPECULATIVE (kill criterion).** A materialized product of $X^\beta$ integers already has $\Theta(X^\beta\log X)$ bits. For $\beta\ge1/2$, $\theta\le1$, merely reading that product prevents the desired strict inequality. A factoring oracle for succinct straight-line-program inputs is a different hypothesis from H1; any positive proposal using one must prove that stronger interface.

## 11. Verification and scope of the answer

**PROVED (finite verification record).** Exact rational arithmetic checks all four rows of the interval-exponent table. Direct enumeration for $2\le N\le2000$ checks the totient-to-radical and divisor-count-to-μ constructions. For odd primes below 80 and $1\le k\le8$, all residues modulo $2^k$ agree with the stated 2-adic root classification. Binomial divisibility is checked through $k=119$. These checks catch arithmetic errors; the preceding arguments provide the proofs. All 26 repository links resolve to existing files.

**PROVED (what is and is not claimed).** The reductions with full proofs here are elementary consequences of the stated interfaces. The combined theorem is a restricted-model counting result with explicit key and signature premises. Neither it nor any cited geometric, generic-ring, or bounded-depth result is a lower bound for unrestricted classical bit algorithms. The short-interval arithmetic establishes the failure of the checked routes to improve prime construction; it does not prove that H1 can never have such a consequence through a future reduction.

**SPECULATIVE (remaining literature gaps).** The Haugland attribution is recorded at abstract level, not upgraded to a checked numbered theorem. The precise historical relativized μ/squarefreeness result requested from Woll was not verified, and no such separation is asserted. Bach’s original numbered abstract statements are distinguished from theorem numbers in its scanned text. These gaps limit the historical catalog; none is used as a premise of the proved reductions or combined theorem.

**PROVED (recommended interpretation).** A candidate factorizer deserves an independent blocker only after its mechanism implies the additional open statement. Fast factoring by itself supplies no contradiction with the analytic or complexity results audited here. The useful obligations are concrete: a global selector with affordable placement, a quantified deterministic order-supply tail, a promise-preserving partial-bit reduction, or a relation sampler with proved supply and fruitfulness.

