**PROVED (proof mechanisms behind the main counting rows).** For degree-one full-cover targets, the lock normalization and curvature bound give at most $C\sqrt{P W_\tau}+C$ covered candidates. Thus

$$
\#\text{covered}\le C\sqrt P\sum_\tau\sqrt{W_\tau}+CX
\le C\sqrt P\,X^{3/2}+CX.
$$

Covering $\asymp P$ candidates forces $X\gg P^{1/3}=N^{1/6}$. Higher-degree support-$k$, degree-$e$ targets have the source bound $CP^{1-e/k}W^{1/k}+C$; Hölder and $k\le2e$, $e\ge2$, give the $N^{1/5}$ branch. T1’s different, true-pair count is proved in §8 rather than replacing actual primes by all candidates.

**PROVED (U’s algebra).** At one modulus with $t_M$ distinct weights, the height count gives $\sum_v1/h(v)\ll\sqrt{t_M}$. Cauchy gives $\sum_v\pi_v^2h(v)\gg u_M^2/\sqrt{t_M}$, $u_M=\sum_v\pi_v$. Hölder over moduli yields $\sum_Mu_M^2/\sqrt{t_M}\ge\lambda^2/\sqrt{TJ}$. Insert WIN and $T\le X$, $\sum\bar w\le X^2$; then $X^{5/2}\gg P\lambda^2/\sqrt J$. Using $J\le T\le X$ gives the second bound. The inequalities do not manufacture WIN.

**PROVED (L’s scope).** Independently draw each $x_j$ uniformly outside $S_j$. Avoiding the forbidden set shifts the mean of a bounded statistic by at most $2|S_j|/q$. Hoeffding plus a union bound controls all $Q$ statistics with positive probability at the stated scale; the condition $J^2\le q/4$ leaves positive probability of distinctness as well. These fake zero-hit configurations need not satisfy the actual reciprocal-prime identities. That last distinction is the available escape.

### 7.2 Ward–Maestro floors

**KNOWN (restricted models).** [Ward–Maestro, *Floors for deterministic integer factoring*](https://igent.ai/research/floors-not-bounds/factoring_report.pdf), Sept. 2026:

| Scope | Assumptions ⇒ floor; exact locator |
|---|---|
| **KNOWN: cells** | Positive integer forms $aq+bp$; real-range coverage; tested integer sets $T_i$, $W_i=\vert T_i\vert $. Materialized $T\ge m+\sum_i\lceil W_i/m\rceil$; free $T\ge m+\sum_iW_i/m$. Floors $N^{1/5},N^{1/6}$: Definition 2.1, Theorems 3.4/3.6. |
| **KNOWN: thinning/robustness** | Enumerated residue hypotheses, modulus $q\le N^{1/20}$: $N^{1/5}(\varphi(q)/q)^{3/5}$, Theorem 2.8. Fixed-degree forms: Theorem 9.4; adaptive cell-membership coverage: Proposition 9.5. |
| **KNOWN: explicit differences** | Explicit $B-G$ cover; start-statistic envelope $D_{\max}\le r^\eta$: $N^{(1-\eta)/(5-4\eta)}$, Theorem 4.4. Polynomial-size divisibility objects: $N^{1/4}$, Proposition 4.5. |
| **KNOWN: pieces/localization** | Separate pieces priced $\ge\sqrt{W_j}$, $H=\max(1,\max_j\sum_{i\in I_j:\,a_i\le b_i\le\lceil Ca_i\rceil}1/a_i)$: $N^{1/4}/\sqrt H$, Theorem 10.1. M1, $N^{1/4}\le L\le\sqrt N$: $\min(LN^{-1/4}/\operatorname{polylog}N,L^{3/5}N^{-1/10})$, Theorem 13.2; position/depth refinement, Theorem 13.15. |
| **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE: NFS** | Uniform root, $H<\min(p,q)$: probability $\le d^2(2H+1)^{d+1}/N$, Proposition 38.1. Coprime common-root resultant floor: Proposition 40.1. Equidistributed-root **heuristic**: $\min(\Vertf\Vert_\infty m)=N^{(2d-1)/(d^2+d-1)+o(1)}$; GNFS-constant optimum, Propositions 39.1/42.1. |
| **KNOWN: other parts** | Fixed-list ECM exposure criterion: Proposition 32.4, not a universal lower bound. Common-($p-1,q-1$) algorithms: Proposition 28.2; positive-density $p-1$ success: Proposition 35.3. |

**PROVED (independent resultant inequality).** If coprime integer polynomials $f,g$ of degrees $d,e$ share a root modulo $N$, their nonzero resultant is divisible by $N$: the Sylvester resultant identity specializes to zero at that root. Hadamard’s determinant bound gives

$$
N\le|\operatorname{Res}(f,g)|
\le (d+1)^{e/2}(e+1)^{d/2}\|f\|_\infty^e\|g\|_\infty^d.
$$

This elementary inequality survives fast factoring. It constrains these explicit polynomial pairs, not all ways of finding a factor. A bound on coefficients alone does not prohibit a different relation architecture or improve an analytic smoothness estimate.

**PROVED (how to read this together with the repository).** The supplied notes’ full-cover and local-pair models are different quantifiers. D′ can beat a materialized single-family floor while obeying a broader $1/6$ floor. Supplied factor information changes the search problem, as the Coppersmith example below illustrates. A faster algorithm that never constructs the tested cell families has no obligation to violate their geometric lemmas.

### 7.3 Which known algorithm families have which escapes?

The labels in the running-time column separate established algorithms from unproved analyses. “Outside” means the model’s definition does not contain that method, not that a theorem has been disproved.

| Family | Coverage/locality and information | Relations/order information | Determinism and bound |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pollard–Strassen / BGS products | **PROVED.** Divisibility by interval products, not a degree-one true-factor selector. Dense degree grows; product trees give a square-root saving in interval length. | **PROVED.** Direct nonunit detection; no need for smooth $p-1$. | **KNOWN.** Deterministic $N^{1/4+o(1)}$ general bound; see Bostan–Gaudry–Schost, SIAM J. Comput. 36 (2007), **Theorem 5** ([primary paper](https://mathexp.eu/bostan/publications/BoGaSc07.pdf)) for the evaluation ingredient, as used in [Track C §1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/trackC_analysis.md). It supplies no sub-$1/6$ general algorithm. |
| Harvey / Harvey–Hittmeir | **KNOWN.** Explicit degree-one collision targets and arithmetic thinning. Harvey, Math. Comp. 90 (2021), **Theorem 1.1**; HH, *A log-log speedup for exponent one-fifth deterministic integer factorisation*, Math. Comp. 91 (2022), **Theorem 1.1**. | **PROVED.** Collision identity uses $p-1$, but not only a fixed list of smooth-order annihilators. | **KNOWN.** Worst-case exponent $1/5$; the refinement improves logarithms. [Harvey](https://doi.org/10.1090/mcom/3658). |
| D′ / AV | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Many-modulus selective coverage, within T1 after the stated budget domination. | **PROVED.** Shared collision machinery; average coverage and extraction, not merely a fixed smooth-order list. | **PROVED in supplied notes.** Deterministic $N^{1/6}\operatorname{polylog}N$ mean on uniform $S_n$, with $N^{1/5}$ fallback; [AV](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/avgtime_results.md), [D′](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/dcltheorem_results.md). It attains, rather than escapes, the local floor. |
| Pollard $p-1$, ECM | **PROVED.** Success is group-order/point-order divisibility, outside cell coverage. ECM varies curves. | **PROVED.** One-sided annihilation must separate the two prime components. Fixed-list certificates are covered by §8; input-dependent choices need not be. | **SPECULATIVE at the usual subexponential ECM bound.** Its familiar expected $L_p[1/2,\sqrt2]$ analysis needs the relevant smooth-order supply. A positive-density $p-1$ theorem is not an almost-all or worst-case factorizer. |
| QS / NFS | **PROVED.** Relation collection and global linear algebra, outside the charged sumset-cover definition. | **PROVED.** Build a square congruence from dependencies; fruitfulness is an additional issue. | **SPECULATIVE.** The standard QS $L[1/2]$, GNFS $L[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}]$, and straightforward deterministic enumerations with those costs rely on relation-supply/fruitfulness assumptions. A heuristic deterministic candidate family can have the desired structural signature; it is not a proved deterministic bound. |
| Schnorr–Lenstra / Seysen / LP | **KNOWN.** Quadratic-class-group relation methods are outside local Lehman coverage. Seysen, Math. Comp. 48 (1987), **Proposition 8.6 / Theorem 8.7**, gives the subgroup-based extraction context; LP’s **§1 main theorem** gives the unconditional randomized $L[1/2,1]$ bound. | **PROVED.** Smooth ideals, relations, and ambiguous forms; not an identity $C_N^{h(p,q)}=D_N$ with a known short true-factor exponent. | **KNOWN / SPECULATIVE.** LP’s randomized bound is rigorous; the earlier analyses have their own GRH or smoothness assumptions. Deterministically enumerating the random choices does not preserve the expectation bound. |
| Coppersmith | **KNOWN.** Given roughly $\frac14\log_2N$ high or low bits of a factor, polynomial-time lattice decoding factors $N=pq$: Coppersmith, J. Cryptology 10 (1997), **Theorems 4–5**. These are outside full-range cell coverage. [Article](https://doi.org/10.1007/s001459900030). | **PROVED.** Uses arithmetic smallness and lattices, not only group orders. | **PROVED conditional use.** Polynomial decoding with a sufficiently precise supplied hint is not a polynomial algorithm that obtains the hint. |
| Shor | **KNOWN.** Quantum order finding and measurement, outside classical collision accounting and classical generic-query bounds. Shor, SIAM J. Comput. 26 (1997), **§§4–5, order-finding/factoring algorithms**. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/9508027). | **PROVED.** Supplies an exact order via a quantum procedure, then uses the classical order-to-factor reduction. | **KNOWN.** Randomized quantum polynomial time; not a classical deterministic escape. |

**PROVED (intersection of necessary escapes).** For a classical deterministic factorizer with worst-case cost $N^{1/6-\delta}$, certified local T1/T2/T3 coverage cannot remain its sole success certificate. If it still uses cell coverage, it must also leave the appropriate full-cover/cost model; if it uses the O1/A1 sketches, it must leave their linear/dense-representation premises. These are **conditional membership tests**, not a demand that every algorithm break every row. A relation algorithm can avoid the entire cell family. A group-only algorithm can avoid cell locality yet still face an unresolved deterministic order-supply or separation problem.

**SPECULATIVE (which family is closest).** No family in this table has a **proved classical deterministic** worst-case or balanced-almost-all $N^{1/6-\delta}$ guarantee. QS/NFS are the clearest structurally compatible candidates; LP shows that a relation/class-group escape can already be made rigorous with randomness. Shor meets a stronger speed goal by changing the computational model. This is an audit conclusion about the listed families, not a proof that an unlisted family cannot work.

